1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:00,933
It is not.

2
00:00:00,933 --> 00:00:05,127
You already did the hard part, the job description, back in Section 1.4.

3
00:00:05,127 --> 00:00:09,845
What is left is mechanical, and this lecture is where you prove that to yourself.

4
00:00:09,845 --> 00:00:12,816
Open a blank n8n canvas and click "Add first step".

5
00:00:12,816 --> 00:00:17,593
The very first choice you get is a trigger, and for this agent that trigger is one

6
00:00:17,593 --> 00:00:21,554
thing: a new email arriving. n8n calls the node Email Trigger, IMAP.

7
00:00:21,554 --> 00:00:25,923
Why does every build start at the trigger rather than the interesting part?

8
00:00:25,923 --> 00:00:30,175
Because n8n will not let you start anywhere else, and its reason is worth

9
00:00:30,175 --> 00:00:33,903
internalising: a workflow is a sentence that begins with "when".

10
00:00:33,903 --> 00:00:35,185
When an email arrives.

11
00:00:35,185 --> 00:00:36,233
When Monday comes.

12
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:40,835
Until you have chosen the when, there is nothing for any later box to react to.

13
00:00:40,835 --> 00:00:45,554
People who sketch the clever middle first end up with logic that has no doorbell.

14
00:00:45,554 --> 00:00:49,923
Choose the doorbell first and the rest of the build has somewhere to stand.

15
00:00:50,473 --> 00:00:55,091
This is a shorter configuration screen than most people expect from their first

16
00:00:55,091 --> 00:00:55,383
node.

17
00:00:55,383 --> 00:01:00,235
Two visible settings, and one button: "Set up credential." That button is doing the

18
00:01:00,235 --> 00:01:00,936
actual work.

19
00:01:00,936 --> 00:01:05,788
Look at the Mailbox Name field and recognise it, because you have met it before, on

20
00:01:05,788 --> 00:01:06,138
paper.

21
00:01:06,138 --> 00:01:10,289
This is the scope line of the job description turned into a form field.

22
00:01:10,289 --> 00:01:15,023
Leave it on the default and the agent reads everything that lands anywhere in the

23
00:01:15,023 --> 00:01:15,374
inbox.

24
00:01:15,374 --> 00:01:19,700
Type the name of one folder, the way Priya types Renewals, and the trigger

25
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,447
physically cannot see past that folder's walls.

26
00:01:22,447 --> 00:01:27,182
One field, and the difference between "reads my email" and "reads the one shelf I

27
00:01:27,182 --> 00:01:27,883
pointed at".

28
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:32,501
If your provider does folders as labels, same move, the label's name goes here.

29
00:01:32,501 --> 00:01:37,119
Spend the thirty seconds to set it now, even on a practice inbox, purely so the

30
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:39,107
habit exists before the stakes do.

31
00:01:39,657 --> 00:01:44,142
Gmail, Outlook and most providers publish these settings on a single support page,

32
00:01:44,142 --> 00:01:48,354
and most ask you to generate an app password rather than use your normal one.

33
00:01:48,354 --> 00:01:52,894
That is a good sign, not a hurdle: it means a leaked n8n credential cannot log into

34
00:01:52,894 --> 00:01:56,669
your actual account settings, only read the mailbox you scoped it to.

35
00:01:56,669 --> 00:02:01,154
An app password, if you have never made one, is a spare key your provider cuts for

36
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:05,585
one specific app: a long generated string, shown to you once, that works for mail

37
00:02:05,585 --> 00:02:10,016
access and nothing else, and that you can cancel on its own without changing your

38
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:10,782
real password.

39
00:02:10,782 --> 00:02:14,556
Generating one takes about a minute on your provider's security page.

40
00:02:14,556 --> 00:02:19,097
At Calloway and Finch the mail runs on a company server, so Priya's version of this

41
00:02:19,097 --> 00:02:23,692
step was one message to the office IT contact asking for the host name, the port and

42
00:02:23,692 --> 00:02:25,442
an app password for her account.

43
00:02:25,442 --> 00:02:29,982
If that is your situation too, that one sentence email is the whole step, and it is

44
00:02:29,982 --> 00:02:31,952
a normal request, not a strange one.

45
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:36,492
Leave the SSL toggle on wherever you see it: it simply means the connection travels

46
00:02:36,492 --> 00:02:40,923
encrypted, and every provider expects it. [SCREENSHOT-NEEDED: your own provider's

47
00:02:40,923 --> 00:02:45,463
app-password screen, since that page belongs to your account and cannot be captured

48
00:02:45,463 --> 00:02:46,119
generically]

49
00:02:46,669 --> 00:02:48,459
That last point is deliberate.

50
00:02:48,459 --> 00:02:53,410
A trigger with nothing downstream is not a mistake, it is exactly where every build

51
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:53,828
starts.

52
00:02:53,828 --> 00:02:58,779
But do not leave the lecture without watching the trigger actually catch something.

53
00:02:58,779 --> 00:03:03,611
Use the node's fetch test option to pull a recent email through, and look at what

54
00:03:03,611 --> 00:03:08,204
appears: one row, with the sender, the subject and the body in named columns.

55
00:03:08,204 --> 00:03:12,141
That is the wire table habit from Section 0 paying its first rent.

56
00:03:12,141 --> 00:03:16,794
If the fetch comes back empty, the folder you named has no mail in it, so send

57
00:03:16,794 --> 00:03:19,181
yourself a test message and fetch again.

58
00:03:19,181 --> 00:03:24,191
Thirty seconds, and you have seen with your own eyes exactly what the next lecture's

59
00:03:24,191 --> 00:03:25,444
agent will be handed.

60
00:03:25,444 --> 00:03:29,501
Next lecture connects the piece that actually reads what lands here.
